[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Working with Headers in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"HTTP headers are an essential part of web communication. They allow the server to send additional information to the client (usually a web browser) about the response. In PHP, you can manipulate these headers to control various aspects of the client's behavior, such as redirection, content type, caching, and more."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Sending Headers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `header()` function is used to send raw HTTP headers. It must be called before any actual output is sent to the browser (before any HTML tags, blank lines from PHP, etc.)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
// Redirect to another page
header('Location: https://www.example.com/');
exit; // Always exit after a redirect to prevent further script execution
?&gt;"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the `header('Location: ...')` function sends a 'Location' header, instructing the browser to redirect to the specified URL. The `exit;` statement is crucial to stop the script from further processing and sending any other output, which could interfere with the redirect."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Content Type"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use headers to specify the content type of the response. This is important for ensuring that the browser interprets the data correctly."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
// Set the content type to JSON
header('Content-Type: application/json');

$data = array('name' =&gt; 'John Doe', 'age' =&gt; 30);
echo json_encode($data);
?&gt;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example sets the 'Content-Type' header to 'application/json', indicating that the response body contains JSON data. The `json_encode()` function converts the PHP array into a JSON string."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Status Codes"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also set HTTP status codes using headers. This is useful for indicating the success or failure of a request."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
// Set a 404 Not Found status code
header('HTTP/1.0 404 Not Found');
echo 'Page not found';
?&gt;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example sets the HTTP status code to 404, indicating that the requested resource was not found."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Caching Headers"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Headers can be used to control how the browser caches the response. This can improve performance by reducing the number of requests to the server."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
// Set caching headers to expire in 1 hour
header('Cache-Control: max-age=3600');
header('Expires: ' . gmdate('D, d M Y H:i:s', time() + 3600) . ' GMT');
?&gt;"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example sets the 'Cache-Control' header to 'max-age=3600', indicating that the response can be cached for 3600 seconds (1 hour). The 'Expires' header is also set to the same time, but in a specific date format."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Headers must be sent before any output. Once the browser receives any output (even a single space), it's too late to send headers."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use `header()` function carefully, as incorrect headers can lead to unexpected behavior."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always `exit;` after a redirect to prevent further script execution."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be mindful of character encoding when setting headers, especially for 'Content-Type'."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Checking if Headers Have Been Sent"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use the `headers_sent()` function to check if headers have already been sent. This is useful for preventing errors when trying to send headers after output has already been sent."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"&lt;?php
if (headers_sent()) {
    echo 'Headers have already been sent.';
} else {
    header('Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8');
    echo 'Headers sent successfully.';
}
?&gt;"}]
